    "result_text": "The implementation is complete and the full test suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`, 1 test, 0 failures).\n\n## Summary\n\nThe spec required emitting an audit line via the logger named `AUDIT` on successful owner creation, containing the owner id, `customerCode`, and `registrationDate`.\n\n**Changes:**\n\n1. **New `OwnerAuditLogger`** (`audit/OwnerAuditLogger.java`) \u2014 a small, single-responsibility Spring `@Component` that owns the audit-trail concern. It holds the dedicated `AUDIT` SLF4J logger and exposes `ownerCreated(Owner)`, keeping audit formatting out of the business logic:\n   ```\n   AUDIT.info(\"Owner created: id={} customerCode={} registrationDate={}\", ...)\n   ```\n\n2. **`ClinicServiceImpl`** \u2014 injected `OwnerAuditLogger` via the constructor and called `ownerAuditLogger.ownerCreated(owner)` immediately after `ownerRepository.save(owner)` in `createOwner`, so the audit fires only on a successful persist (when the id, customerCode, and registrationDate are all populated).\n\nThe emitted line (e.g. `AUDIT - Owner created: id=11 customerCode=TOW-SUR-0001 registrationDate=2026-09-21`) contains all three required values, and `LocalDate.toString()` matches the ISO string returned by `GET /api/owners/{id}`, satisfying the test's `anyContains(id, customerCode, registrationDate)` assertion.\n\nI placed the logic in a dedicated collaborator rather than inlining it in the service, so the service method stays focused on the create rules and the audit responsibility lives in one reusable unit.",
